      "bodyTr": "Yong Hye-in, a Basic Income Party lawmaker nominated as minister of gender equality and family affairs, refuted criticism directed at her yesterday and stated her intention to maintain both her ministerial position and National Assembly seat. She argued that concurrent service as a lawmaker and government minister is permitted by law, and questioned whether the convention requiring proportional representation lawmakers to resign upon becoming minister—unlike district representatives—is merely politicians dividing up positions among themselves.\n\nHowever, this argument is nothing more than sophistry that distorts the fundamental principles of representative democracy and the essence of the proportional representation system. District representatives and proportional representation lawmakers have different representational mandates. District representatives are elected by voters in their constituencies through direct voting based on the candidate's character and platform. When a district representative resigns due to ministerial duties or other reasons, the constituency loses its representative and a by-election must be held. In contrast, proportional representation seats are allocated based on the vote share earned by the party, not the individual candidate. Since voters choose based on the party's policies, values, and nomination order, when a proportional lawmaker resigns, the next person on the proportional list can assume the seat without creating a vacancy.\n\nYong's refusal to resign from her legislative seat is merely a calculation to maintain the Basic Income Party's status as a parliamentary party. This is because if she resigns, her proportional seat would be taken by a Democratic Party member. Furthermore, Yong described her nomination as appointing an opposition ruling party lawmaker as minister, which makes no sense. Yong was not elected as a Democratic Party candidate. She was elected as a proportional representation candidate for the Democratic Party's satellite party, the Together Democratic Union, in 2024, then returned to the Basic Income Party through a \"loophole expulsion\" procedure. Thanks to this, the Basic Income Party is classified as a parliamentary party receiving government subsidies. Her initial election in 2020 followed the same pattern. Everyone knows this history, yet she claims she was nominated as an opposition lawmaker. Is this a model case of bipartisan cooperation? Moreover, she compared this relationship to the \"DJ-KP alliance,\" suggesting an exaggerated self-assessment beyond reason. Yong should seriously consider why even within the ruling bloc there are calls for her inevitable resignation or even removal. In an era where fairness is paramount, can a politician who has benefited from loopholes and tricks be deemed a suitable minister for the Republic of Korea?",
