        "bodyTr": "Democratic Party leader Kim Min-seok has named People Power Party lawmaker Lee Jin-sook and independent lawmaker Han Dong-hun, openly displaying his determination to obstruct their parliamentary activities. Leader Kim, whose notebook bearing the words \"Lee Jin-sook and Han Dong-hun OUT\" was exposed to the media two days ago and sparked controversy over political theatrics, reiterated yesterday that \"these two should not be in the National Assembly,\" revealing strong hostility. While this could be dismissed as typical political offensive between ruling and opposition parties, what is more troubling is that he has proposed specific legislative amendments to achieve this. This suggests a dangerous notion that the majority party in the National Assembly can control even the qualifications of opposition lawmakers.\n\nThe Democratic Party passed a resolution calling for the expulsion of lawmaker Lee Jin-sook at a plenary session on the 26th of last month, citing controversy over distortion and denigration of the May 18 Democratic Uprising. However, actual expulsion requires the approval of more than two-thirds of seated members as stipulated in the Constitution, making it difficult with only ruling bloc votes. This is why the Democratic Party has devised an alternative: suspending lawmakers' qualifications. Currently, the National Assembly Act allows only up to 90 days of attendance suspension as punishment, requiring a quorum of seated members and a majority vote among those present. The party plans to amend this to enable suspension of parliamentary activities for up to one year. With the party leader publicly mentioning amendments to the National Assembly Act, there is nothing preventing the Democratic Party from pushing this through as party policy. For a dominant ruling party to use its force to drive out minority opposition lawmakers runs counter to parliamentary democracy and represents dangerous thinking.\n\nShould the National Assembly Act be amended according to the Democratic Party's intentions, not only these two lawmakers but all opposition members would face potential danger of having their qualifications suspended under pressure from the majority party holding over half the seats. Proper parliamentary activities would become impossible. Using the majority party's arbitrary power to prevent opposition lawmakers' parliamentary activities for extended periods infringes on the rights of voters who elected those representatives. National Assembly members can face disciplinary action for misconduct. However, this must be done only after clearly establishing facts and holding them accountable according to law and procedure. Designing severe punishment by relying on a majority is an unavoidable target of criticism as a political abuse of legislative power. Was it not precisely to prevent majority tyranny that the Constitution set such a high threshold of two-thirds for expulsion of lawmakers?",
